@<Module@/Basic@=
{
    "description": "Some common structures and filters."
}
@

Executes the input as a script, the input must have a shebang,
use the output of that script as the output.

@<Basic@/evaluate@=#!/bin/sh
set -e
temp_file=`mktemp`
cat - > $temp_file
chmod +x $temp_file
$temp_file
rm $temp_file
@
@<Module@/Text Processing@=
{
    "description": "Text processing based on python."
}
@
@<Text Processing@/remove newlines@=
@<.@/basic python settings@>
lines = sys.stdin.buffer.read().split(b'\n')
if lines[-1] == b'':
    lines.pop()
sys.stdout.buffer.write(b' '.join(lines))
@
Strip every newline at the end of the input, so the text joins the line after its reference.
Carriage returns of CRLF endings are stripped too; other trailing whitespace is kept.

@<Text Processing@/remove ending newlines@=
@<.@/basic python settings@>
sys.stdout.buffer.write(sys.stdin.buffer.read().rstrip(b'\r\n'))
@
@<Module@/Invocation@=
{
    "description": "Command line syntax shared by the structures of every language."
}
@
The usage lines list every usage of the command, one per line, after "Usage: ".
@<Invocation@/command line usage@=
@<Text Processing@/basic python settings@>
@<.@/invocation syntax helpers@>


def usage_line(usage):
    words = [syntax.name]
    words += [f'[{option_with_value(opt)}]' for opt in usage.options if not opt.hide]
    words += map(placeholder, usage.arguments)
    return ' '.join(words)


syntax = configuration(InvocationSyntax, r'''
@<.@/invocation syntax@>
''')
print('Usage: ' + '\n       '.join(map(usage_line, syntax.usages)))
@|Basic@/evaluate@
The manpage synopsis shows the usage lines in roff, one paragraph line per usage.
@<Invocation@/manpage synopsis@=
@<Text Processing@/basic python settings@>
@<.@/invocation syntax helpers@>
@<.@/manpage helpers@>


def usage_lines(usage):
    lines = [f'.B {roff(syntax.name)}']
    lines += [f'[{bold_option(opt)}]' for opt in usage.options if not opt.hide]
    lines += [italic(placeholder(arg)) for arg in usage.arguments]
    return lines


syntax = configuration(InvocationSyntax, r'''
@<.@/invocation syntax@>
''')
print('\n.br\n'.join('\n'.join(usage_lines(usage)) for usage in syntax.usages))
@|Basic@/evaluate@
The manpage options describe every option in a tagged paragraph, hidden ones included.
@<Invocation@/manpage options@=
@<Text Processing@/basic python settings@>
@<.@/invocation syntax helpers@>
@<.@/manpage helpers@>


def option_entry(opt):
    names = [f'-{opt.short}'] if opt.short else []
    names.append(LONG_PREFIX + opt.name)
    value = '' if opt.type == 'bool' else ' ' + italic(metavar(opt))
    tag = ', '.join(rf'\fB{roff(name)}\fR' for name in names) + value
    return f'.TP\n{tag}\n{roff(opt.description)}\n'


syntax = configuration(InvocationSyntax, r'''
@<.@/invocation syntax@>
''')
print(''.join(map(option_entry, all_options(syntax))), end='')
@|Basic@/evaluate@
A text processing script is a Python script that declares its dependencies:
jsonnet evaluates configuration, and pydantic validates it.
When python3 cannot import them, the script runs itself again through uv,
which provides them from its cache, downloading them on the first run.
The marker variable stops a second attempt, and is removed so nested scripts start afresh.
@<Text Processing@/basic python settings@=
#!@<.@/Internals@/path to python interpreter@>
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.10"
# dependencies = ["jsonnet>=0.20", "pydantic>=2"]
# ///
import os
import re
import sys
from typing import Literal

try:
    import _jsonnet
    import pydantic
except ImportError:
    if os.environ.pop('LITAR_UV_RUN', None):
        raise
    os.environ['LITAR_UV_RUN'] = '1'
    os.execvp('uv', ['uv', 'run', '--quiet', '--script', *sys.argv])
os.environ.pop('LITAR_UV_RUN', None)

@<.@/configuration helpers@>
@
Configuration is a Jsonnet expression, so a block can extend an object of a base node with +.
A pydantic model validates the evaluated value.
An invalid configuration, like any fail, prints a message and fails the evaluation.
@<Text Processing@/configuration helpers@=
def fail(message):
    sys.exit(message)


def configuration(model, source):
    try:
        value = _jsonnet.evaluate_snippet('configuration', source)
    except RuntimeError as error:
        fail(str(error))
    try:
        return model.model_validate_json(value)
    except pydantic.ValidationError as error:
        fail(str(error))
@
@<Text Processing@/Internals@/path to python interpreter@=
#!/bin/sh
which python3
@|Basic@/evaluate@
The structures include this chunk in their generators,
so every generator validates the invocation syntax the same way.
A short name is one letter or digit.
@<Invocation@/invocation syntax helpers@=
class Option(pydantic.BaseModel):
    model_config = pydantic.ConfigDict(extra='forbid')
    name: str = pydantic.Field(pattern=r'^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$')
    short: str | None = pydantic.Field(None, pattern=r'^[0-9A-Za-z]$')
    type: Literal['bool', 'string', 'int', 'int64', 'uint', 'uint64', 'float64'] = 'bool'
    argument: str | None = None
    default: bool | int | float | str | None = None
    description: str = ''
    hide: bool = False

    @pydantic.model_validator(mode='after')
    def check_default(self):
        value = self.default
        if value is None:
            return self
        if self.type == 'bool':
            suits = isinstance(value, bool)
        elif self.type == 'string':
            suits = isinstance(value, str)
        elif self.type == 'float64':
            suits = isinstance(value, (int, float)) and not isinstance(value, bool)
        else:
            suits = (isinstance(value, int) and not isinstance(value, bool)
                     and (value >= 0 or not self.type.startswith('u')))
        if not suits:
            raise ValueError(f'default {value!r} does not suit type {self.type}')
        return self
@
Options and arguments at the top level become one usage named after the command.
Usage names, option names, and short names are each unique in the whole command,
because its parser reads the options of every usage.
@<Invocation@/invocation syntax helpers@+
class Usage(pydantic.BaseModel):
    model_config = pydantic.ConfigDict(extra='forbid')
    name: str
    options: list[Option] = []
    arguments: list[str] = []


class InvocationSyntax(pydantic.BaseModel):
    model_config = pydantic.ConfigDict(extra='forbid')
    name: str = 'command'
    usages: list[Usage] = pydantic.Field(min_length=1)

    @pydantic.model_validator(mode='before')
    @classmethod
    def gather_single_usage(cls, data):
        if isinstance(data, dict) and 'usages' not in data:
            data = dict(data)
            usage = {'name': data.get('name', 'command')}
            for key in ('options', 'arguments'):
                if key in data:
                    usage[key] = data.pop(key)
            data['usages'] = [usage]
        return data

    @pydantic.model_validator(mode='after')
    def check_unique_names(self):
        options = all_options(self)
        for kind, names in (
            ('usage', [usage.name for usage in self.usages]),
            ('option', [opt.name for opt in options]),
            ('short option', [opt.short for opt in options if opt.short]),
        ):
            for name in names:
                if names.count(name) > 1:
                    raise ValueError(f'{kind} {name!r} is given twice')
        return self
@
A placeholder stands for a value in a usage line: upper case, with underscores between words.
@<Invocation@/invocation syntax helpers@+
def all_options(syntax):
    return [opt for usage in syntax.usages for opt in usage.options]


def placeholder(name):
    return name.upper().replace('-', '_').replace(' ', '_')


def metavar(opt):
    return placeholder(opt.argument or opt.name)
@
The invocation syntax of a command is one Jsonnet object.
The usage lines and the manpage of the command are generated from it here,
and a structure turns it into the parser of its language,
so every form of the command line comes from this one chunk.
A usage is one way to call the command: its options and its positional arguments.
A command with one usage may give its options and arguments at the top level instead.
An option is a switch when its type is bool; any other type takes a value,
shown by its argument name, or by its own name, in upper case.
The type is bool, string, int, int64, uint, uint64, or float64,
and a default must suit it. A hidden option is left out of usage lines.
A structure rejects the features its language does not support.

{
    name: COMMAND_NAME,
    usages: [
        {
            name: USAGE_NAME,
            options: [
                {
                    name: LONG_NAME,
                    short: SHORT_NAME (optional),
                    type: TYPE (optional, bool by default),
                    argument: ARGUMENT_NAME (optional),
                    default: DEFAULT (optional),
                    description: DESCRIPTION (optional),
                    hide: HIDDEN_FROM_USAGE (optional),
                },
                ...
            ],
            arguments: [
                ARGUMENT,
                ...
            ],
        },
        ...
    ],
}

@<Invocation@/invocation syntax@=
{}
@
An option is shown by its short name if it has one, otherwise by its long name.
@<Invocation@/invocation syntax helpers@+
LONG_PREFIX = r'''
@<.@/long option prefix@>
'''.strip()


def option_name(opt):
    return f'-{opt.short}' if opt.short else LONG_PREFIX + opt.name


def option_with_value(opt):
    return option_name(opt) + ('' if opt.type == 'bool' else f' {metavar(opt)}')
@
A long option starts with this prefix in usage lines and the manpage.
A structure whose parser reads long options after another prefix overrides it under its own label.
@<Invocation@/long option prefix@=
--
@
roff escapes a backslash as \e and a hyphen as \-,
so option names render with minus signs that can be copied into a shell.
@<Invocation@/manpage helpers@=
def roff(text):
    return text.replace('\\', r'\e').replace('-', r'\-')


def italic(text):
    return rf'\fI{roff(text)}\fR'


def bold_option(opt):
    text = rf'\fB{roff(option_name(opt))}\fR'
    return text if opt.type == 'bool' else text + ' ' + italic(metavar(opt))
@
